dict(pas="14.1", w="憲=xiàn/Xian — Yuan Xian's personal name | 問/asked about | 恥。/shame | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^邦/(when) the state | 有/has | 道，=dào/the Way | 穀；/(to draw) a salary | ^邦/(when) the state | 無/lacks | 道，=dào/the Way | 穀，/(to draw) a salary | ^恥/shameful | 也。」/(it is)",
  l="Xian asked about shame.\nThe Master said: “When the state has the Way — a salary;\nwhen the state lacks the Way — a salary:\nthat is shameful.”",
  i="Xian asked what is shameful. The Master said: “To do nothing but draw an official's pay while the state is on the Way, and to go on drawing it when the state has lost the Way — both are a disgrace.”",
  n="The book is named 憲問 from its opening words; Zhu Xi's heading quotes Hu Yin (胡氏): this book was perhaps recorded by Yuan Xian, and it has forty-seven chapters (此篇疑原憲所記，凡四十七章). 憲 xiàn is Yuan Xian 原憲, the Yuan Si of 6.3 (Zhu Xi: 憲原思名); he is named by his personal name, as a man names himself — perhaps why Hu Yin thought the book his record. 穀 'grain' is an officer's pay, reckoned in grain (Zhu Xi: 穀禄也), as in 8.12. The two halves are bare: 'state has the Way — salary; state lacks the Way — salary', and 恥也 'shameful' sums up. Zhu Xi takes both as shameful: when the state has the Way, to be unable to do anything worth doing, and when it lacks the Way, to be unable to keep oneself good alone — knowing only how to eat one's salary — are both a disgrace. Xian, guarded and upright (狷介), surely knew already that a salary under a lawless state is shameful, but not that one under a well-ruled state can be; so the Master took his question and spoke of both, to widen his aim and spur him to do something. He Yan's Kong Anguo reads the first half the other way: when the state has the Way one should eat its salary; it is taking a salary at the court of a lord without the Way that is shameful. Compare 8.13, where poverty under a well-ruled state is the shame."),
# ---------------- 14.2 ----------------
dict(pas="14.2", w="克、/the urge to win | 伐、/boasting | 怨、/resentment | 欲/(and) desire | ^不/not | 行=xíng/carried out | 焉，/in (him) | ^可/can (this) | 以/thereby | 為=wéi/count as | 仁/humaneness | 矣？/(is it so)",
  l="The urge to win, boasting, resentment and desire\nnot carried out in him —\ncan that count as humaneness?",
  i="If a man keeps the urge to get the better of others, boastfulness, resentment and greed from ever showing in what he does, may that be called humaneness?",
  n="Yuan Xian asks again — Zhu Xi: this too is Yuan Xian asking from what he himself could do (此亦原憲以其所能而問也). The line has no speaker and no 曰, and is left unquoted. Four nouns, each a fault, then 不行焉 'are not carried out in him'. Zhu Xi: 克 is loving to win (好勝), 伐 boasting of oneself (自矜), 怨 anger and grudge (忿恨), 欲 greed (貪欲). He Yan's Ma Rong (his text writes 剋): loving to get the better of others, boasting of one's achievements, petty grudges, greed. 可以為仁 'can thereby count as humaneness', 為 wéi; 矣 states the conclusion he hopes he has reached, and the Master's reply shows it is put as a question."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^可/(it) can | 以/thereby | 為=wéi/count as | 難=nán/hard | 矣，/(so it is) | ^仁/humaneness | 則/then | 吾/I | 不/do not | 知=zhī/know | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said: “It can count as hard;\nas for humaneness, I do not know.”",
  i="The Master said: “That may be called difficult. Whether it is humaneness, I cannot say.”",
  n="可以為難 'can count as hard' (為 wéi, 難 nán) answers his 可以為仁 word for word, changing only the last word. 仁則吾不知也 'humaneness — then I do not know': 則 after a topic sets it against what went before, and 'I do not know' is the Master's way of withholding the name, as 5.4 and 5.7. Zhu Xi: to have these four and be able to check them so they cannot act may be called hard; but humaneness is Heaven's principle whole and undivided, free of the burden of the four of itself — 'not carried out' is not enough to speak of it. He quotes Master Cheng: to be without the four is something only the humane can do; to have them and master the feelings so they do not act is also hard to do, but to call it humaneness is not right — here the sage opened the matter deeply, and it is a pity Xian could not ask again. The comment then takes up an objection — is not keeping the four from acting the 'overcoming the self' (克己, 12.1), the way to seek humaneness? No: overcoming the self's selfishness to return to the rites leaves no selfish desire behind and recovers Heaven's principle as it is; merely to check them and not act has no thought of pulling up the root of the sickness, and lets it lie hidden in the breast. He Yan's Bao Xian: these four are hard to keep from acting, but that is not yet humaneness."),
# ---------------- 14.3 ----------------
dict(pas="14.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^士/an officer | 而/and yet | 懐=huái/cherishing | 居，/(his) comforts | ^不/is not | 足/enough | 以/thereby | 為=wéi/to count as | 士/an officer | 矣。」/(so it is)",
  l="The Master said: “An officer who cherishes his comforts\nis not fit to count as an officer.”",
  i="The Master said: “A man of service who hankers after the comforts of home does not deserve the name.”",
  n="士 is the officer, the man of service, as 4.9. 而 'and yet' joins the noun to a verb phrase: 'an officer, and (yet) cherishing…'. 懐 huái, the edition's form of 懷, is 'to cherish, to hold in one's heart', as 4.11 君子懷德. 居 is more than 'home': Zhu Xi: wherever one's thoughts find ease and comfort (意所便安處也). 不足以為士 'not enough to count as an officer', 為 wéi. He Yan's comment (unnamed): an officer should set his heart on the Way and not seek ease; one who cherishes his dwelling is no officer."),
# ---------------- 14.4 ----------------
dict(pas="14.4", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^邦/(when) the state | 有/has | 道，=dào/the Way | ^危/lofty | 言/(in) speech | 危/lofty | 行；=xíng/(in) conduct | ^邦/(when) the state | 無/lacks | 道，=dào/the Way | ^危/lofty | 行=xíng/(in) conduct | 言/(in) speech | 孫。」=xùn/humble",
  l="The Master said: “When the state has the Way,\nlofty in speech, lofty in conduct;\nwhen the state lacks the Way,\nlofty in conduct, humble in speech.”",
  i="The Master said: “When the state is on the Way, speak high-mindedly and act high-mindedly. When it has lost the Way, still act high-mindedly, but speak with care and deference.”",
  n="危 here is not 'in peril' but 'high, steep' (Zhu Xi: 危高峻也) — words and deeds that stand tall; He Yan's Bao Xian takes it as 'stern' (厲): when the state has the Way one may be stern in word and deed. 孫 is read xùn and is the same word as 遜 'yielding, humble' (Zhu Xi: 孫卑順也); He Yan's text writes 遜. Zhu Xi marks both 行 and 孫 去聲: for 行 'conduct' that is xìng, a reading modern practice does not keep (we print xíng); for 孫 it is xùn. Zhu Xi quotes Yin Tun (尹氏): the gentleman's holding of himself may not change, but in speech there are times when he dares not say everything, to escape disaster — and so for one who governs a state to bring its officers to humble speech is surely perilous. The comment that follows in He Yan (unnamed): conduct stays stern and does not follow the crowd; speech yields, to keep harm far off."),
# ---------------- 14.5 ----------------
dict(pas="14.5", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^有/having | 徳/virtue | 者/the one | 必/surely | 有/has | 言，/words | ^有/having | 言/words | 者/the one | 不/does not | 必/necessarily | 有/have | 徳；/virtue | ^仁/humane | 者/the one who is | 必/surely | 有/has | 勇，/courage | ^勇/brave | 者/the one who is | 不/does not | 必/necessarily | 有/have | 仁。」/humaneness",
  l="The Master said: “One who has virtue surely has words;\none who has words does not necessarily have virtue;\nthe humane one surely has courage;\nthe brave one does not necessarily have humaneness.”",
  i="The Master said: “A man of virtue will always have something worth saying, but one who has plenty to say is not always a man of virtue. The humane man will always have courage, but the brave man is not always humane.”",
  n="Two pairs of one shape: 有X者必有Y 'one who has X surely has Y', then the reverse with 不必 'not necessarily'. 徳 is the edition's form of 德. Zhu Xi: one who has virtue has harmony and order stored within, and its flower shows outside; one who can speak may be merely glib and quick of tongue (便佞口給). The humane have no selfish burden on the heart, and seeing what is right they will surely do it; the brave may have only the strength of blood and breath (血氣之强). He quotes Yin Tun: one with virtue surely has words, but one merely able to speak need not have virtue; the humane man's will is surely brave, but one merely brave need not have humaneness. He Yan's one comment (unnamed): virtue cannot be got at by guessing, so it must show itself in words."),
